 `-D warnings` in debug and `-O`, so a non-zero rustc exit is a failure — the
 reverse of avoiding the shape.
 
+## clippy `-D warnings` is a gate, not a metric
+
+Two clippy runs with two different jobs:
+
+| run | flags | job |
+|---|---|---|
+| the metric | `-W clippy::pedantic` | counts every pedantic finding by lint name and ratchets it (`IDIOM_BASELINE`) |
+| the gate | `-W clippy::pedantic -A <UNAVOIDABLE_PEDANTIC> -D warnings` | **fails the fixture** on any finding outside that list, in debug *and* `-O`; `rust_fuzz` runs the same check over every generated program in both profiles |
+
+The exclusion list is ONE constant, `rust_backend::UNAVOIDABLE_PEDANTIC`, with
+ONE entry: `clippy::too_many_lines` — an emitted `fn` is exactly as long as the
+script it came from (`22_vars_store`'s `main` is 332 lines, the native ERE
+matcher 142, `__sh_printf` 109), and shortening it means chunking a
+straight-line body into synthetic sub-functions, which cannot preserve `let`
+scoping, `break`/`return` targets, `'unwind` blocks or redirect guards. Nothing
+else is suppressed anywhere, no `#[allow(` is emitted, and the metric keeps
+counting that lint, so the number is not narrowed either. What each family the
+gate caught was fixed by, in the emitter:
+
+| finding | emitted fix |
+|---|---|
+| `unreadable_literal` | `group_digits`: a long literal is emitted `2_000_000_000` / `9_223_372_036_854_775_807i64` |
+| `manual_let_else`, `single_match_else` | `let Ok(mut ch) = process.spawn() else { … };` instead of `let mut ch = match …` |
+| `map_unwrap_or` | `ch.wait().map_or(1, \|s\| s.code().unwrap_or(1))` |
+| `if_not_else` | the loop status store is `if ran { last } else { 0 }`; a non-empty test is the named predicate `__sh_nonempty` |
+| `format_push_string` | `let _ = write!(body, "\\x{:02x}", c)` with a function-local `use std::fmt::Write as _;` |
+| `needless_pass_by_value` | `fn __sh_v_set_s(name: &str, v: impl Into<String>)` — the value is consumed |
+| `explicit_iter_loop` | `for ch in &mut children` |
+| `match_same_arms` | `Ok(0) \| Err(_) => break,` |
+| `many_single_char_names` | helper bindings are named (`text`, `idx`, `kont`, `chars`, `out`), not `s`/`i`/`k`/`c` |
+| `cast_sign_loss`, `cast_possible_wrap` | `i64::try_from(len).unwrap_or(i64::MAX)` / `usize::try_from(p).unwrap_or(0)` instead of `as` |
+| `uninlined_format_args` | `format!("{fv:.p2$}")`, `format!("{piece}{}", …)`, plus the scanner fix below |
+
+The last one is the kind of defect the gate exists to find: `inline_format_args`
+met a CHAR literal (`'"'`) in an emitted escape helper, treated it as the start
+of a string, and silently stopped inlining anywhere after that line — 21's
+pipeline stage format and 22's loop-status formats had been left uninlined for
+that reason, not by choice. The scanner now skips char literals.
+
